<section xml:id="sec-tree-diagrams" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Tree Diagrams</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        3.0 Statistics and Probability
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        3.2 Probability III
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Calculate probabilities using tree diagrams
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>


    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Sheet title: Tree diagrams (limited to a maximum of three branches; each branch allowed only two possible outcomes)
         Content summary: Tree diagrams for combined events (maximum three branches, two outcomes per branch).
         Suggested activity: Learners draw tree diagrams for two-stage experiments, such as picking two pens from a box with and without replacement.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Tree diagram with sliders for branch probabilities; the probability of each outcome is computed along the branches. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLO: Essential (d); lesson 5.2 also carries (e), the key inquiry question revisited and the curriculum links. Lessons 5.1 and 5.2 as subsections.
         Scope read as: at most three stages, exactly two outcomes at every branch point (text, exercises, STACK, interactive).
         Activity: as planning notes (two pens with and without replacement) in lesson 5.1 | changed: lesson 5.2 adds its own activity (three traffic lights)
         JSXGraph: interactive-tree-diagram-explorer (assets/jsxgraph/tree-diagram-explorer.js), as planning notes
         STACK: assets/stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/16-probability-iii/05-tree-diagrams/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 5.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-two-stage-tree-diagrams">
        <title>Two-Stage Tree Diagrams</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a tree diagram shows each stage; branches from one point add to 1; multiply along a path and add between paths; with replacement the branches repeat; without replacement count what is left; a second stage can depend on the first.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> draw and read two-stage tree diagrams.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> pens, snacks and vaccinating calves.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners compare picking two pens with and without replacement.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Give each group a box of <m>6</m> pens, <m>4</m> blue and <m>2</m> red. Do the first round with replacement and the second without. Draw both trees on the board side by side. Critical thinking: ask what changes at the second stage, and why.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-picking-two-pens">
            <title>Picking Two Pens</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A box holds <m>6</m> pens: <m>4</m> blue and <m>2</m> red. Two pens are taken at random, one after the other.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>The first pen is put back before the second is taken (with replacement). Draw a tree diagram and find the probability that both pens are blue.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Both stages have branches <m>\frac{4}{6}</m> blue and <m>\frac{2}{6}</m> red. <m>P(\text{both blue}) = \frac{4}{6} \times \frac{4}{6} = \frac{4}{9}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Now the first pen is not put back (without replacement). What is left in the box after a blue pen is taken? After a red pen? Draw the tree and find the probability that both are blue.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>After blue: <m>3</m> blue and <m>2</m> red, so <m>\frac{3}{5}</m> and <m>\frac{2}{5}</m>. After red: <m>4</m> blue and <m>1</m> red, so <m>\frac{4}{5}</m> and <m>\frac{1}{5}</m>. <m>P(\text{both blue}) = \frac{4}{6} \times \frac{3}{5} = \frac{12}{30} = \frac{2}{5}</m> (see <xref ref="fig-pens-tree"/>).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Without replacement, find the probability of one pen of each colour.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Blue then red or red then blue: <m>\frac{8}{30} + \frac{8}{30} = \frac{16}{30} = \frac{8}{15}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Add the probabilities at the ends of all four paths of your second tree. What do you get?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>\frac{12}{30} + \frac{8}{30} + \frac{8}{30} + \frac{2}{30} = 1</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To set out the rules for drawing and using tree diagrams.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> without replacement, the second-stage branches were found by counting what was left.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Write a probability on every branch.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Two checks: each pair of branches from one point adds to 1; all the paths add to 1.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-tree-diagrams">
            <title>Tree Diagrams</title>
            <p>
                A <term>tree diagram</term> shows an experiment in <term>stages</term>. Each branch carries a probability, and each route from the start to an end is a <term>path</term>.
                <ul>
                    <li><p>The branches from one point add to 1.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Multiply along a path to find the probability of that path.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Add the paths that make up an event. All the paths together add to 1.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>With replacement</term>, every stage has the same branches. <term>Without replacement</term>, count what is left before writing the next stage's branches. In general a second-stage branch may depend on what happened at the first stage, as in <xref ref="example-vaccinating-calves"/>. The trees in this book have at most three stages and exactly two outcomes at each point.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-pens-tree">
            <caption>Two pens taken without replacement from <m>4</m> blue and <m>2</m> red.</caption>
            <image width="70%">
                <shortdescription>A two-stage tree. First pen blue 4/6 or red 2/6. After blue: blue 3/5, red 2/5. After red: blue 4/5, red 1/5. Path probabilities: blue blue 12/30, blue red 8/30, red blue 8/30, red red 2/30.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small]
\draw (0.00,0.00) -- (2.80,1.60) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{4}{6}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (2.85,1.60) {Blue};
\draw (0.00,0.00) -- (2.80,-1.60) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{2}{6}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (2.85,-1.60) {Red};
\draw (4.10,1.60) -- (6.90,2.40) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{3}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (6.95,2.40) {Blue};
\draw (4.10,1.60) -- (6.90,0.80) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (6.95,0.80) {Red};
\draw (4.10,-1.60) -- (6.90,-0.80) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{4}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (6.95,-0.80) {Blue};
\draw (4.10,-1.60) -- (6.90,-2.40) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{1}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (6.95,-2.40) {Red};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.00,2.40) {BB: $\frac{12}{30}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.00,0.80) {BR: $\frac{8}{30}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.00,-0.80) {RB: $\frac{8}{30}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.00,-2.40) {RR: $\frac{2}{30}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-tree-diagram-explorer">
            <caption>Drag the sliders. The branch pairs always add to 1, each path multiplies its branches, and the four paths always add to 1.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-tree-diagram-explorer" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="16:12" source="jsxgraph/tree-diagram-explorer.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-tree-diagram-explorer" surface="jsxboard" aspect="16:12"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Drag <alert>P(A)</alert> to set the first-stage branch to <m>A</m>. The other branch shows <m>1</m> minus it.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Drag <alert>P(B after A)</alert> and <alert>P(B after not A)</alert> to set the second-stage branches. The four path probabilities and <m>P(B)</m> update as you drag.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Tick <alert>same second stage</alert> to make the second stage the same after <m>A</m> and after not <m>A</m> (independent stages).</p></li>
                            <li><p>With <alert>same second stage</alert> not ticked, set <alert>P(A)</alert> to <m>0.8</m>, <alert>P(B after A)</alert> to <m>0.05</m> and <alert>P(B after not A)</alert> to <m>0.4</m>. Check that <m>P(B) = 0.12</m>, as in <xref ref="example-vaccinating-calves"/>.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-vaccinating-calves">
            <title>Vaccinating Calves</title>
            <statement>
                <p>On a farm in Nyandarua, <m>0.8</m> of the calves are vaccinated against East Coast fever. A vaccinated calf catches the disease with probability <m>0.05</m>; a calf that is not vaccinated catches it with probability <m>0.4</m>. A calf is chosen at random. Find the probability that it (a) is vaccinated and catches the disease, (b) catches the disease, (c) does not catch the disease.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <figure xml:id="fig-calves-tree">
                    <caption>Vaccination and East Coast fever.</caption>
                    <image width="70%">
                        <shortdescription>A two-stage tree. Vaccinated 0.8, not vaccinated 0.2. After vaccinated: ill 0.05, not ill 0.95. After not vaccinated: ill 0.4, not ill 0.6. Path probabilities 0.04, 0.76, 0.08 and 0.12.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small]
\draw (0.00,0.00) -- (2.80,1.60) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0.8$};
\node[anchor=west] at (2.85,1.60) {Vaccinated};
\draw (0.00,0.00) -- (2.80,-1.60) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0.2$};
\node[anchor=west] at (2.85,-1.60) {Not vaccinated};
\draw (5.30,1.60) -- (8.10,2.40) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0.05$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.15,2.40) {Ill};
\draw (5.30,1.60) -- (8.10,0.80) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0.95$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.15,0.80) {Not ill};
\draw (5.30,-1.60) -- (8.10,-0.80) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0.4$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.15,-0.80) {Ill};
\draw (5.30,-1.60) -- (8.10,-2.40) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0.6$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.15,-2.40) {Not ill};
\node[anchor=west] at (9.70,2.40) {$0.04$};
\node[anchor=west] at (9.70,0.80) {$0.76$};
\node[anchor=west] at (9.70,-0.80) {$0.08$};
\node[anchor=west] at (9.70,-2.40) {$0.12$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>(a) Multiply along the path: <m>0.8 \times 0.05 = 0.04</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) Two paths end in <q>ill</q>: <m>0.8 \times 0.05 + 0.2 \times 0.4 = 0.04 + 0.08 = 0.12</m>.</p>
                <p>(c) <m>1 - 0.12 = 0.88</m>, or add the other two paths: <m>0.76 + 0.12 = 0.88</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-snacks-without-replacement">
            <title>Snacks Without Replacement</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A tin holds <m>8</m> snacks: <m>5</m> mandazi and <m>3</m> chapati. Wafula takes two at random without replacement. Find the probability that he takes (a) two mandazi, (b) one of each, (c) at least one chapati.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>After a mandazi is taken, <m>4</m> mandazi and <m>3</m> chapati are left; after a chapati, <m>5</m> mandazi and <m>2</m> chapati.</p>
                <p>(a) <m>\dfrac{5}{8} \times \dfrac{4}{7} = \dfrac{20}{56} = \dfrac{5}{14}</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) <m>\dfrac{5}{8} \times \dfrac{3}{7} + \dfrac{3}{8} \times \dfrac{5}{7} = \dfrac{15}{56} + \dfrac{15}{56} = \dfrac{15}{28}</m>.</p>
                <p>(c) Everything except two mandazi: <m>1 - \dfrac{5}{14} = \dfrac{9}{14}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-walking-to-school-early">
            <title>Walking to School Early</title>
            <statement>
                <p>In a Grade 12 class in Nyeri, <m>40\%</m> of the learners walk to school. Three quarters of those who walk, and half of the others, arrive before 7.30 a.m. A learner is chosen at random. Find the probability that the learner arrives before 7.30 a.m.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>First stage: walks <m>0.4</m>, does not walk <m>0.6</m>. Second stage: early <m>\frac{3}{4}</m> after walking and <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> otherwise.</p>
                <p><m>P(\text{early}) = 0.4 \times \dfrac{3}{4} + 0.6 \times \dfrac{1}{2} = 0.3 + 0.3 = 0.6</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type a fraction with a slash, for example <c>5/12</c>, and a decimal with a point, for example <c>0.35</c>. Give probabilities exactly: do not round.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-completing-a-two-stage-tree-diagram">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-completing-a-two-stage-tree-diagram" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/16-probability-iii/05-tree-diagrams/Completing-a-two-stage-tree-diagram.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-two-draws-without-replacement">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-two-draws-without-replacement" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/16-probability-iii/05-tree-diagrams/Two-draws-without-replacement.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-two-stage-trees">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-tomato-pests">
                <statement><p>The probability that a tomato plant is attacked by pests is <m>0.3</m>. Two plants grow in separate shambas, independently. Draw a tree diagram and find the probability that (a) both are attacked, (b) exactly one is attacked, (c) neither is attacked.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>0.09</m> (b) <m>0.42</m> (c) <m>0.49</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-mangoes-without-replacement">
                <statement><p>A basket holds <m>6</m> green and <m>4</m> yellow mangoes. Two are taken at random without replacement. Find the probability that (a) both are green, (b) there is one of each colour, (c) both are yellow.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> (b) <m>\frac{8}{15}</m> (c) <m>\frac{2}{15}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>(a) <m>\frac{6}{10} \times \frac{5}{9} = \frac{1}{3}</m>. (b) <m>2 \times \frac{6}{10} \times \frac{4}{9} = \frac{8}{15}</m>. (c) <m>\frac{4}{10} \times \frac{3}{9} = \frac{2}{15}</m>. Check: <m>\frac{5}{15} + \frac{8}{15} + \frac{2}{15} = 1</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-busy-market-day">
                <statement><p>The probability of rain on market day is <m>0.3</m>. If it rains, the probability that the market is busy is <m>0.4</m>; if it does not rain, it is <m>0.8</m>. Find the probability that the market is busy.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>0.68</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>0.3 \times 0.4 + 0.7 \times 0.8 = 0.12 + 0.56 = 0.68</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-cards-one-to-seven">
                <statement><p>Seven cards are numbered 1 to 7. Two are taken at random without replacement. Find the probability that (a) both numbers are odd, (b) one is odd and one is even.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>\frac{2}{7}</m> (b) <m>\frac{4}{7}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>There are four odd and three even cards. (a) <m>\frac{4}{7} \times \frac{3}{6} = \frac{2}{7}</m>. (b) <m>\frac{4}{7} \times \frac{3}{6} + \frac{3}{7} \times \frac{4}{6} = \frac{4}{7}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-find-the-blue-counters">
                <statement><p>A bag holds <m>3</m> red counters and <m>n</m> blue counters. Two counters are drawn with replacement. The probability that both are red is <m>\frac{9}{64}</m>. Find <m>n</m>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>n = 5</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>\left(\frac{3}{3 + n}\right)^2 = \frac{9}{64}</m>, so <m>\frac{3}{3 + n} = \frac{3}{8}</m> and <m>n = 5</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-wekesa-two-red">
                <title>Challenge: Find the Mistake</title>
                <statement><p>A bag holds <m>2</m> red and <m>3</m> white beads. Two are taken without replacement. Wekesa says the probability that both are red is <m>\left(\frac{2}{5}\right)^2 = \frac{4}{25}</m>. Find his mistake and the correct answer.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>What is left in the bag after a red bead is taken?</p></hint>
                <answer><p>He worked as if the first bead were put back. The answer is <m>\frac{1}{10}</m>.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>After a red bead is taken, <m>1</m> red and <m>3</m> white are left, so the second branch is <m>\frac{1}{4}</m>: <m>\frac{2}{5} \times \frac{1}{4} = \frac{1}{10}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-two-stage-trees">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give tree skeletons with blank labels, and use the explorer. Focus on the activity and <xref ref="example-vaccinating-calves"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-tomato-pests"/> and <xref ref="ex-busy-market-day"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-tomato-pests"/> to <xref ref="ex-find-the-blue-counters"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-wekesa-two-red"/>. Extension: show that, with the activity's <m>4</m> blue and <m>2</m> red pens, one pen of each colour is more likely without replacement than with replacement. (<m>\frac{8}{15}</m> against <m>2 \times \frac{4}{6} \times \frac{2}{6} = \frac{4}{9}</m>.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 5.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-three-stage-tree-diagrams">
        <title>Three-Stage Tree Diagrams</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> a tree with three stages has eight paths; <q>exactly two</q> collects several paths; <q>at least one</q> is found by the complement.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> draw and use three-stage trees, for independent stages and without replacement.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> traffic lights, guessing, a three-part journey; value probability in daily life.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners extend the tree diagram to three stages.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>First ask for a guess of the probability that all three lights are green. Then draw the tree together. Point out that real traffic lights are often linked, so independence is a model.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-three-traffic-lights">
            <title>Three Traffic Lights</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Nafula's matatu passes three traffic lights on the way to town. Each light is green when the matatu reaches it with probability <m>0.6</m> and red otherwise, independently.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Draw a tree diagram with three stages. How many paths are there?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Eight paths, from GGG to RRR (see <xref ref="fig-traffic-lights-tree"/>).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the probability that all three lights are green.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>0.6^3 = 0.216</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the probability that no light is green.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>0.4^3 = 0.064</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the probability that exactly one light is red.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Paths GGR, GRG, RGG, each <m>0.6 \times 0.6 \times 0.4 = 0.144</m>: <m>3 \times 0.144 = 0.432</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Find the probability that at least one light is red.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Everything except GGG: <m>1 - 0.216 = 0.784</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To extend the tree rules to three stages.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> part (d) collected three paths; part (e) used the complement.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Count the paths for <q>exactly</q>; use the complement for <q>at least one</q>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Without replacement, the numbers go down by one at every stage.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-three-stage-trees">
            <title>Three-Stage Trees</title>
            <p>
                A tree with three stages and two outcomes at each point has <m>2 \times 2 \times 2 = 8</m> paths. The same rules apply: multiply along a path, and add the paths you need. For <q>exactly two</q>, add the three paths with two successes. For <q>at least one</q>, use <m>1 - P(\text{none})</m>: the only path left out is the one with no successes.
            </p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-traffic-lights-tree">
            <caption>Three traffic lights, each green (G) with probability <m>0.6</m> and red (R) with probability <m>0.4</m>.</caption>
            <image width="75%">
                <shortdescription>A three-stage tree with branches G 0.6 and R 0.4 at every point. The eight paths and their probabilities: GGG 0.216, GGR 0.144, GRG 0.144, GRR 0.096, RGG 0.144, RGR 0.096, RRG 0.096, RRR 0.064.</shortdescription>
