        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-guessing-three-questions">
            <title>Guessing Three Questions</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Kerubo guesses the answers to three multiple-choice questions, each with four options. Find the probability that she gets (a) all three right, (b) none right, (c) exactly one right, (d) at least one right.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>Each guess is right with probability <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> and wrong with probability <m>\frac{3}{4}</m>, independently.</p>
                <p>(a) <m>\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^3 = \frac{1}{64}</m>. (b) <m>\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^3 = \frac{27}{64}</m>.</p>
                <p>(c) Three paths (RWW, WRW, WWR), each <m>\frac{1}{4} \times \frac{3}{4} \times \frac{3}{4} = \frac{9}{64}</m>: <m>3 \times \frac{9}{64} = \frac{27}{64}</m>.</p>
                <p>(d) <m>1 - \frac{27}{64} = \frac{37}{64}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-three-pens">
            <title>Three Pens Without Replacement</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A box holds <m>7</m> pens: <m>4</m> blue and <m>3</m> red. Three are taken at random without replacement. Find the probability that (a) all three are blue, (b) exactly two are blue, (c) at least one is red.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <figure xml:id="fig-three-pens-tree">
                    <caption>Three pens taken without replacement from <m>4</m> blue (B) and <m>3</m> red (R).</caption>
                    <image width="75%">
                        <shortdescription>A three-stage tree without replacement. First pen B 4/7 or R 3/7. Second pen after B: B 3/6, R 3/6; after R: B 4/6, R 2/6. Third pen after BB: B 2/5, R 3/5; after BR or RB: B 3/5, R 2/5; after RR: B 4/5, R 1/5. Path probabilities out of 210: BBB 24, BBR 36, BRB 36, BRR 24, RBB 36, RBR 24, RRB 24, RRR 6.</shortdescription>
                        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small]
\draw (0.00,0.00) -- (2.20,2.40) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{4}{7}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (2.25,2.40) {B};
\draw (0.00,0.00) -- (2.20,-2.40) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{3}{7}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (2.25,-2.40) {R};
\draw (2.70,2.40) -- (4.90,3.60) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{3}{6}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (4.95,3.60) {B};
\draw (2.70,2.40) -- (4.90,1.20) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{3}{6}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (4.95,1.20) {R};
\draw (2.70,-2.40) -- (4.90,-1.20) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{4}{6}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (4.95,-1.20) {B};
\draw (2.70,-2.40) -- (4.90,-3.60) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{2}{6}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (4.95,-3.60) {R};
\draw (5.40,3.60) -- (7.60,4.20) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,4.20) {B};
\draw (5.40,3.60) -- (7.60,3.00) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{3}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,3.00) {R};
\draw (5.40,1.20) -- (7.60,1.80) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{3}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,1.80) {B};
\draw (5.40,1.20) -- (7.60,0.60) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,0.60) {R};
\draw (5.40,-1.20) -- (7.60,-0.60) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{3}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,-0.60) {B};
\draw (5.40,-1.20) -- (7.60,-1.80) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,-1.80) {R};
\draw (5.40,-3.60) -- (7.60,-3.00) node[pos=0.5, above=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{4}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,-3.00) {B};
\draw (5.40,-3.60) -- (7.60,-4.20) node[pos=0.5, below=1pt, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$\frac{1}{5}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (7.65,-4.20) {R};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,4.20) {BBB: $\frac{24}{210}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,3.00) {BBR: $\frac{36}{210}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,1.80) {BRB: $\frac{36}{210}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,0.60) {BRR: $\frac{24}{210}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,-0.60) {RBB: $\frac{36}{210}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,-1.80) {RBR: $\frac{24}{210}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,-3.00) {RRB: $\frac{24}{210}$};
\node[anchor=west] at (8.45,-4.20) {RRR: $\frac{6}{210}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>(a) <m>\dfrac{4}{7} \times \dfrac{3}{6} \times \dfrac{2}{5} = \dfrac{24}{210} = \dfrac{4}{35}</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) Paths BBR, BRB and RBB each have probability <m>\dfrac{36}{210}</m>, so the answer is <m>\dfrac{108}{210} = \dfrac{18}{35}</m>.</p>
                <p>(c) Everything except BBB: <m>1 - \dfrac{4}{35} = \dfrac{31}{35}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-three-part-journey">
            <title>A Three-Part Journey</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Kiprono's journey to work has three parts: a boda boda, which is on time with probability <m>0.9</m>; a matatu, on time with probability <m>0.8</m>; and a train, on time with probability <m>0.7</m>. Assume the three parts are independent. Find the probability that (a) all three are on time, (b) exactly one is late, (c) at least one is late.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>(a) <m>0.9 \times 0.8 \times 0.7 = 0.504</m>.</p>
                <p>(b) Only the boda boda late: <m>0.1 \times 0.8 \times 0.7 = 0.056</m>. Only the matatu: <m>0.9 \times 0.2 \times 0.7 = 0.126</m>. Only the train: <m>0.9 \times 0.8 \times 0.3 = 0.216</m>. Total <m>0.398</m>.</p>
                <p>(c) <m>1 - 0.504 = 0.496</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <paragraphs xml:id="paragraphs-probability-in-our-lives">
            <title>Probability in Our Lives</title>
            <p>
                Probability helps people plan when the future is uncertain. A weather forecast of a <m>70\%</m> chance of rain helps a farmer decide when to plant. The germination rate printed on a seed packet tells the farmer how many seeds to buy. Insurance companies use probabilities to set the price of cover for a matatu or a shamba. Road-safety campaigns compare the probability of serious injury with and without a helmet or a seatbelt, to persuade people to travel more safely.
            </p>
            <p>
                Look back at the question at the start of this chapter: why is probability important in our daily lives? Write two sentences in your own words.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type a fraction with a slash, for example <c>5/12</c>, and a decimal with a point, for example <c>0.35</c>. Give probabilities exactly: do not round.</p>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-three-stage-tree-diagrams-with-equal-probabilities">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-three-stage-tree-diagrams-with-equal-probabilities" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/16-probability-iii/05-tree-diagrams/Three-stage-tree-diagrams-with-equal-probabilities.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="ex-finding-the-mistake-in-a-three-stage-tree-calculation">
            <!--<title></title>-->
            <stack label="stk-finding-the-mistake-in-a-three-stage-tree-calculation" source="stack/top/3-statistics-and-probability/16-probability-iii/05-tree-diagrams/Finding-the-mistake-in-a-three-stage-tree-calculation.xml"/>
        </exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-three-stage-trees">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-three-coins-tree">
                <statement><p>Use a tree diagram to find the probability of exactly two heads when three coins are tossed. Compare with your list in <xref ref="ex-three-coins-listing"/>.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>\frac{3}{8}</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-die-three-times">
                <statement><p>A die is rolled three times. Find the probability of (a) three sixes, (b) no six, (c) at least one six.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>\frac{1}{216}</m> (b) <m>\frac{125}{216}</m> (c) <m>\frac{91}{216}</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-three-bean-seeds">
                <statement><p>Three bean seeds are planted. Each germinates with probability <m>0.8</m>, independently. Find the probability that (a) all three germinate, (b) exactly two germinate, (c) at least one germinates.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>0.512</m> (b) <m>0.384</m> (c) <m>0.992</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>(a) <m>0.8^3 = 0.512</m>. (b) <m>3 \times 0.8 \times 0.8 \times 0.2 = 0.384</m>. (c) <m>1 - 0.2^3 = 0.992</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-choosing-a-committee">
                <statement><p>Three learners are chosen at random from <m>5</m> girls and <m>3</m> boys to form a committee. Find the probability that (a) all three are girls, (b) exactly two are girls.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>\frac{5}{28}</m> (b) <m>\frac{15}{28}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>(a) <m>\frac{5}{8} \times \frac{4}{7} \times \frac{3}{6} = \frac{60}{336} = \frac{5}{28}</m>. (b) Three paths (GGB, GBG, BGG), each <m>\frac{60}{336}</m>: <m>\frac{180}{336} = \frac{15}{28}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-tea-factory-machines">
                <statement><p>A tea factory has three machines that work on a given day with probabilities <m>0.95</m>, <m>0.9</m> and <m>0.8</m>, independently. Find the probability that (a) all three work, (b) at least one fails.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>(a) <m>0.684</m> (b) <m>0.316</m></p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-probability-in-the-news">
                <statement><p>Find two places where probability is used in Kenya, for example a weather forecast on radio or television, or a road-safety message. Write each as a probability statement and say what decision it helps someone make.</p></statement>
                <answer><p>Answers vary. A good answer gives the source, a statement such as <q>the probability of rain in Kisumu tomorrow is <m>0.7</m></q>, and a decision, such as whether to plant or to carry an umbrella.</p></answer>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-more-green-than-red">
                <title>Challenge: More Green Than Red</title>
                <statement><p>In <xref ref="act-three-traffic-lights"/>, find the probability that the matatu meets more green lights than red.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Which paths have more G than R?</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>0.648</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Three green (GGG, <m>0.216</m>) or exactly two green (GGR, GRG, RGG, <m>3 \times 0.144 = 0.432</m>): <m>0.216 + 0.432 = 0.648</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>


        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Curriculum Links</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Key Inquiry Question</title><p>Why is probability important in our daily lives?</p></li>
                <li><title>Core Competencies</title><p>Critical thinking, through drawing and checking tree diagrams; communication and collaboration, through the card sort in <xref ref="act-choosing-safe-transport"/> and the pooled class data in <xref ref="act-tossing-bottle-tops"/>; digital literacy, through the simulator, the tree explorer and <xref ref="ex-probability-in-the-news"/>.</p></li>
                <li><title>Values</title><p>Social justice (equity), through fair draws and fair games in <xref ref="sec-theoretical-probability"/>; respect, through group work and listening to other views.</p></li>
                <li><title>Pertinent and Contemporary Issues</title><p>Road safety, in <xref ref="act-choosing-safe-transport"/>, the helmet examples and the seatbelt exercise.</p></li>
                <li><title>Learning Outcome</title><p>This lesson carries the outcome on appreciating probability in daily life, through the paragraphs above and <xref ref="ex-probability-in-the-news"/>.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-three-stage-trees">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a printed eight-path tree to label. Focus on <xref ref="example-guessing-three-questions"/> and <xref ref="example-three-part-journey"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-three-coins-tree"/> and <xref ref="ex-three-bean-seeds"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-three-coins-tree"/> to <xref ref="ex-probability-in-the-news"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-more-green-than-red"/>. Extension: draw the tree for <q>stop counting as soon as a light is red</q>; it has fewer paths. Find the probability that all three lights are green. (<m>0.216</m>: the same path.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

